    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d985">

                <pb facs="0953" n="855" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg855"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Peirce to Mr. Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">St.
                                Petersburg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-10-27"><hi rendition="#italic">October 27,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 133.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your telegram<anchor n="1" xml:id="d985-f1"/> of the 26th instant, a copy of
                    which I append upon the overleaf.</p>
                <p>Acting upon the instructions therein contained, I have requested a further
                    interview with Professor de Martens for the purpose of urging upon him the
                    wishes of our Government that the phrase “and the spirit of international
                    agreements bearing upon the subject” be omitted from the protocol of the
                    arbitration of the claims of American citizens arising out of seizures of
                    American vessels.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herbert H. D.
                    Peirce.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
